\begin{abstract}
Execution-free verification for LLM-based code repair can be formulated in multiple ways, yet no controlled comparison exists across formulations at matched model capacity. We systematically evaluate three formulations: per-test binary classification (\cls{}), execution output generation (\cwm{}), and trajectory-level patch scoring, training on the same source data with formulation-appropriate label granularity (per-test for \cls{}/\cwm{}, variant-level for \traj{}) at controlled model sizes (4B, 8B).

At function level, the three formulations achieve near-equivalent accuracy (\cls{} 88.43\% vs.\ \cwm{} 87.19\% at 4B, 5 seeds; Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) equivalence with trajectory-level at $p{=}0.007$). At repository level (4--8B LoRA fine-tuning), formulation divergence amplifies to 19--26\pp{}, with \cwm{} falling below majority baseline. Cross-model validation with DeepSeek-Coder reveals a formulation $\times$ model interaction: repo-level \cwm{} degradation is consistent across both model families (\cwm{} $\gg$ \cls{} performance drop from function to repo level in both cases), but its magnitude is model-contingent (Qwen3: ${\sim}$5/22\pp{} \cls{}/\cwm{} degradation; DeepSeek: 4--12/25--30\pp{}). \cwm{} additionally exhibits fragility at repo level along two dimensions: format non-compliance (51--74\% unparseable outputs) and higher seed variance at repo level (preliminary; \cls{} variance is model-contingent, ${\sim}$1--8\pp{} across families).

Two findings generalize across conditions. First, per-test classification provides diagnostic granularity (which specific tests fail) at equivalent trajectory-level accuracy at function level, with the advantage concentrating in instances with $\geq$2 failing tests (31\% of instances, 55\% of oracle advantage). Second, formulations exhibit complementary error patterns: an oracle ensemble reaches 90.63\%. \cls{} achieves the highest or equivalent accuracy across conditions, but aggregation penalty and error complementarity motivate complexity-aware routing.
\end{abstract}

tool_args.new_string:
\begin{abstract}
Execution-free verification for LLM-based code repair can be formulated as per-test binary classification (\cls{}), execution output generation (\cwm{}), or trajectory-level patch scoring, yet no controlled comparison exists across formulations. We evaluate all three on the same source data with formulation-appropriate label granularity at controlled model sizes (4--8B LoRA fine-tuning).

At function level, formulations achieve near-equivalent accuracy (\cls{} 88.4\% vs.\ \cwm{} 87.2\% at 4B, 5 seeds; TOST equivalence with trajectory-level at $p{=}0.007$). At repository level, divergence amplifies to 19--26\pp{}, with \cwm{} falling below majority baseline due to format non-compliance and output space complexity. Cross-model validation (Qwen3, DeepSeek-Coder) confirms this pattern is consistent across both families but model-contingent in magnitude.

Two findings generalize. First, per-test classification provides diagnostic granularity (which tests fail) at equivalent trajectory-level accuracy, with the advantage concentrating in multi-failure instances. Second, formulations exhibit complementary errors (oracle ensemble: 90.63\%). \cls{} achieves the highest or equivalent accuracy across conditions, but aggregation penalty and error complementarity motivate complexity-aware routing.
\end{abstract}

